ar yeager rage  show anyone that would listen that  indira gandhi had ordered his plane  destroyed to give uncle sam the finger  in 1975 after a 33-year career general  jaeger retired from the air force his  fame as a pilot had long ago been  eclipsed by that of the astronauts and  he might have faded into a relative  historical footnote where not for the  publication of a book called the right  stuff in 1979 by author and journalist  tom wolf in the book woolf shows that  the achievements of the mercury seven  the original men picked to be astronauts  by nasa would not have gotten off the  ground were not for the groundwork laid  by test pilots such as chuck yeager the  best-selling book was adapted into a  film in 1983 with sam shepard portraying  jaeger on screen jaeger himself appeared  in a cameo role in the critically  acclaimed film which introduced his name  and deeds to a whole new generation of  americans probably never heard of him  jaeger's newfound popularity found him  thrust back into the public in a variety  of ways his autobiography published in  1985 was a bestseller so were a series  of video games published by electronic  arts that featured jaeger's name and  likeness and that he served as a  technical advisor on he was twice  invited to drive the pace car for the  indianapolis 500 and he appeared in a  series of television advertisements for  auto parts company ac delco on a more  somber note jaeger was appointed by  president reagan to the rogers  commission which investigated the  catastrophic destruction of the space  shuttle challenger in january 1986  during a launch watched by millions of  people on live tv the explosion just 73  seconds into the flight killed all seven  astronauts on board including new  hampshire school teacher christa  mcauliffe mcauliffe was supposed to be  the first ordinary person in space the  focus of a massive publicity campaign  that now had turned to disaster over the  course of four months jaeger and the  rest of the commission including former  astronauts neil armstrong and sally ride  held hearings and collected evidence to  uncover what went wrong the commission  found that a critical part inside one of  the boost rockets failed because of  freezing cold weather at the site on the  day of launch this allowed a jet of  white hot burning gas to leak out of the  side of the rocket punching a hole in  the external fuel tank and igniting the  contents blowing the entire shuttle  apart the commission's report was highly  critical of nasa's leadership for  ignoring warnings from engineers and  prioritizing their launch over safety  [Music]  jaeger's post-retirement years were  largely spent in comfort having amassed  a sizable fortune from book royalties  endorsement contracts and speaking  engagements jaeger lived with his wife  glenis in northern california until she  died of ovarian cancer in 1990 after 45  years of marriage the couple had raised  four children together two of which  followed in their father's footsteps  with long careers in the air force  retirement didn't put a damper on the  old test pilot's thirst for speed  however on the 50th anniversary of  jaeger's historic mach 1 flight in 1997  jaeger once again broke the sound  barrier piloting an f15 fighter jet you  think that's incredible just considered  this wasn't even the last time he'd do  this 15 years later at the eye-popping  age of 89 yeager did it again though on  this flight he consented to make the  flight as a passenger the very fact that  an 89 year old man would subject himself  to the rigors of supersonic flight says  a lot about his character not the  jaeger's golden years were free from  controversy however in 2000 the 77 year  old flying ace met and fell in love with  victoria de angelo an actress 35 years  as junior in 2003 they married in a  private ceremony the match caused a rift  between jaeger and his children who  believed that victoria had only married  their father for his money a lawsuit was  filed and then a counter suit and the  litigation dragged on for years in the  end the court sided with jaeger against  his children and jaeger would tell  reporters that he didn't care what his  children thought about his life choices  he was going to do whatever he wanted to  be happy on december 7 2020 chuck yeager  died in los angeles hospital at the age  of 97. the death of this american hero  touched off a wave of mourning  nationwide his body was flown aboard air  force two the vice president of the  united states plane to charleston west  virginia where he was buried with full  military honours in a ceremony that took  place on january the 15th 2021 jaeger  was so well respected that his funeral  briefly brought both republican and  democratic politicians together to pay  their respects often called america's  greatest pilot chuck yeager represents  the bridge between the rough and tumble  aerial cowboys that typified the fighter  pilots of the two world wars with the  highly educated technology savvy  professionals that pilot today's modern  machines though described by tom wolf as  having the right stuff the combination  of guts and daring needed to do things  like break the sound barrier and take  other risks jaeger himself disputed that  he said that his skill in the cockpit  could be attributed to his exceptional  vision a thorough knowledge of his  airplane and how it operated and more  than a little good luck in any case his  contributions to aviation will long be  remembered not only by historians but by  military officers space enthusiasts or  by anybody who watches the books and  films made about his life and  achievements not bad for a humble farm  boy from west virginia  [Music]  you
 

